# Jennifer ORM Guide

## Overview

Jennifer is an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) library for Crystal that provides a clean way to map database tables to Crystal classes and define relationships between them.

## Basic Model Examples

```crystal
# Simple model with relationships and validations
class Contact < Jennifer::Model::Base
  with_timestamps
  mapping(
    id: Primary64,              # Int64 primary key
    name: String,
    gender: {type: String?, default: "male"},
    age: {type: Int32, default: 10},
    description: String?,
    created_at: Time?,
    updated_at: Time | Nil
  )

  has_many :addresses, Address
  has_many :facebook_profiles, FacebookProfile
  has_and_belongs_to_many :countries, Country
  has_one :main_address, Address, {where { _main }}
  has_one :passport, Passport

  validates_inclusion :age, 13..75
  validates_length :name, minimum: 1, maximum: 15
  validates_with_method :name_check

  scope :adult { where { _age > 18 } }
  scope :older { |age| where { _age >= age } }
  scope :ordered { order(name: :asc) }

  def name_check
    if @description && @description.not_nil!.size > 10
      errors.add(:description, "Too large description")
    end
  end
end

# Related models
class Address < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    id: {type: Int64, primary: true},
    main: Bool,
    street: String,
    contact_id: Int64?,
    details: JSON::Any?
  )
  
  validates_format :street, /st\.|street/
  belongs_to :contact, Contact
  
  scope :main { where { _main } }
end

# Model with string primary key
class Passport < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    enn: {type: String, primary: true},
    contact_id: {type: Int64, null: true}
  )

  validates_with EnnValidator
  belongs_to :contact, Contact
end

# Single Table Inheritance (STI) example
class Profile < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    id: Primary64,
    login: String,
    contact_id: Int64?,
    type: String
  )

  belongs_to :contact, Contact
end

class FacebookProfile < Profile
  mapping(uid: String)
  has_and_belongs_to_many :facebook_contacts, Contact, foreign: :profile_id
end

class TwitterProfile < Profile
  mapping(email: String)
end
```

## Mapping Definition

The `.mapping` macro defines model attributes and their database mappings. Use it in two ways:

### Simple syntax
```crystal
field_name: :Type
```

### Named tuple syntax with options
```crystal
field_name: {type: Type, option: value}
```

### Supported Options

| Option | Description | Default |
|--------|-------------|---------|
| `:type` | Crystal data type | Required |
| `:primary` | Mark as primary key | `false` |
| `:null` | Allow nil values | `false` (except primary keys) |
| `:default` | Default value for new objects | None |
| `:column` | Database column name | Field name |
| `:getter` | Generate getter method | `true` |
| `:setter` | Generate setter method | `true` |
| `:virtual` | Virtual field (not persisted) | `false` |
| `:generated` | Database-generated column | `false` |
| `:converter` | Custom serializer/deserializer | None |
| `:auto` | Auto-increment primary key | `true` for Int32/Int64 |

### Making Fields Nullable

Three ways to allow nil values:

```crystal
# Option 1: null parameter
field: {type: String, null: true}

# Option 2: ? syntax
field: String?

# Option 3: Union type
field: String | Nil
```

### Strict Mode

By default, you must define all table fields. To disable this requirement:

```crystal
mapping({
  id: Primary64,
  name: String
}, strict: false)
```

### Generated Methods

The `.mapping` macro automatically generates:

**Class Methods:**
- `.new` - Constructors
- `.field_count` - Number of fields
- `.field_names` - All field names
- `._{{field_name}}` - Query builder helpers
- `.primary` - Primary key criterion
- `.create` / `.create!` - Create and persist objects
- `.build` - Build without saving

**Instance Methods:**
- `#{{field_name}}` - Getter
- `#{{field_name}}=` - Setter
- `#{{field_name}}_changed?` - Track changes
- `#{{field_name}}!` - Non-nil getter (for nullable fields)
- `#{{field_name}}?` - Predicate (for boolean fields)
- `#save` / `#save!` - Persist to database
- `#to_h` / `#to_str_h` - Convert to hash
- `#attribute` / `#set_attribute` - Dynamic access

## Important Notes

⚠️ **Requirements:**
- Every model must have a primary field
- If using multiple mapping macros, Jennifer's mapping must come last

## Relationships

Relationships connect models and simplify database operations. Without relationships, you'd write verbose code:

```crystal
# Without relationships
author = Author.first!
book = Book.create({author_id: author.id, title: "Kobzar"})

# Delete author and books
books = Book.where(author_id: author.id)
books.each(&.destroy)
author.destroy
```

With relationships, operations become simpler:

```crystal
# With relationships
book = author.add_book({title: "Kobzar"})
author.destroy  # Books are destroyed automatically
```

### Relationship Types

Jennifer supports four relationship types:

#### `belongs_to`

Indicates this model's table contains a foreign key reference to another table. Used for one-to-one or one-to-many relationships.

```crystal
class Book < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    id: Primary64,
    author_id: Int64?,
    title: String
  )

  belongs_to :author, Author
end
```

**Generated methods:**
- `#author` - Returns cached author
- `#author_reload` - Reloads and returns author
- `#author_query` - Returns query for author
- `#remove_author` - Removes author
- `#add_author` - Adds author (accepts object or hash)
- `#author!` - Non-nil assertion

**Options:**
- `dependent` - Cleanup strategy (`:none`, `:delete`, `:destroy`, `:restrict_with_exception`)
- `polymorphic` - Enable polymorphic association
- `foreign_type` - Column for polymorphic type
- `required` - Validate presence (boolean or custom message)

```crystal
class Post < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    id: Primary64,
    title: String?,
    user_id: Int64?
  )

  belongs_to :user, User, required: ->(object, _field) do
    "Post #{object.as(Post).title} isn't attached to any user"
  end
end
```

#### `has_one`

Creates a one-to-one relationship where the other model contains the foreign key.

```crystal
class Author < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64)
  
  has_one :biography, Biography, dependent: :destroy
end
```

**Generated methods:** Same as `belongs_to`

**Options:**
- `dependent` - Cleanup strategy (`:nullify`, `:none`, `:delete`, `:destroy`, `:restrict_with_exception`)
- `polymorphic` - Enable polymorphic association
- `foreign_type` - Column for polymorphic type
- `inverse_of` - Specify inverse relationship name

#### `has_many`

Creates a one-to-many relationship.

```crystal
class Author < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64)
  
  has_many :books, Book, dependent: :destroy
end
```

**Generated methods:**
- `#books` - Returns cached collection
- `#books_reload` - Reloads collection
- `#books_query` - Returns query
- `#remove_books` - Removes from collection
- `#add_books` - Adds to collection

**Options:** Same as `has_one`

#### `has_and_belongs_to_many`

Creates a many-to-many relationship through a join table.

```crystal
class Author < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64)
  
  has_and_belongs_to_many :genres, Genre
end

class Genre < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64, name: String)
  
  has_and_belongs_to_many :authors, Author
end
```

**Generated methods:** Same as `has_many`

**Options:**
- `join_table` - Custom join table name
- `association_foreign` - Foreign key on join table

### Bi-directional Associations

Use `inverse_of` to optimize bi-directional relationships:

```crystal
class Author < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64)
  has_many :books, Book, inverse_of: :writer
end

class Book < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64, author_id: Int64?)
  belongs_to :writer, Author, foreign: :author_id
end

# Now objects are properly linked
author = Author.first!
book = author.books.first!
author.object_id == book.writer.object_id # => true
```

### Polymorphic Relationships

Allow a model to belong to multiple other models through a single association:

```crystal
class Picture < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    id: Primary64,
    imageable_type: String?,
    imageable_id: Int64?
  )

  belongs_to :imageable, Union(Employee | Product), polymorphic: true
end

class Employee < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64)
  has_many :pictures, Picture, polymorphic: true, inverse_of: :imageable
end

class Product < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(id: Primary64)
  has_many :pictures, Picture, polymorphic: true, inverse_of: :imageable
end
```

⚠️ **Limitation:** Polymorphic `belongs_to` relations cannot be eagerly loaded:
```crystal
Picture.includes(:imageable) # This will raise an error
```

## Converters

Converters handle custom field serialization. Required methods:

```crystal
module MyConverter
  def self.from_db(rs : DB::ResultSet, options : NamedTuple)
    # Convert from database
  end
  
  def self.to_db(value : T, options : NamedTuple)
    # Convert to database format
  end
  
  def self.from_hash(hash : Hash(String, Jennifer::DBAny | T), 
                     field : String, options : NamedTuple)
    # Convert from hash
  end
end
```

### Built-in Converters

#### TimeZoneConverter
Automatically converts UTC times to application timezone.

Options:
- `time_zone_aware: Bool` - Disable timezone conversion
- `time_format: String` - Parse time strings (default: `%H:%M`)
- `date_format: String` - Parse date strings (default: `%F`)
- `date_time_format: String` - Parse datetime strings (default: `%F %T`)

#### JSONConverter
Handles `JSON::Any` fields automatically.

#### EnumConverter
Maps enums to strings:

```crystal
enum Category
  GOOD
  BAD
end

class Note < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    category: {type: Category?, converter: Jennifer::Model::EnumConverter(Category)}
  )
end
```

#### BigDecimalConverter
For precise decimal operations:

```crystal
class Order < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    total: {
      type: BigDecimal?, 
      converter: Jennifer::Model::BigDecimalConverter(PG::Numeric), 
      scale: 2
    }
  )
end
```

#### JSONSerializableConverter
Maps JSON to Crystal classes:

```crystal
class Location
  include JSON::Serializable
  property latitude : Float64
  property longitude : Float64
end

class User < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    location: {
      type: Location, 
      converter: Jennifer::Model::JSONSerializableConverter(Location)
    }
  )
end
```

## Predefined Mapping Types

Jennifer includes shortcuts for common patterns:

- `Primary32` - Int32 primary key
- `Primary64` - Int64 primary key  
- `Password` - Virtual string field without setter

### Custom Types

Create your own mapping types:

```crystal
class ApplicationRecord < Jennifer::Model::Base
  EmptyString = {type: String, default: ""}
  {% TYPES << "EmptyString" %}
end
```

## Virtual vs Generated Attributes

### Virtual Attributes
Not persisted to database, useful for form fields:

```crystal
class User < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    id: Primary64,
    password_hash: String,
    password: {type: String?, virtual: true},
    password_confirmation: {type: String?, virtual: true}
  )

  validate_confirmation :password
  
  before_create :crypt_password
  
  def crypt_password
    self.password_hash = encrypt(self.password)
  end
end
```

### Generated Attributes
Database-calculated columns (read-only):

```crystal
class User < Jennifer::Model::Base
  mapping(
    id: Primary64,
    first_name: String,
    last_name: String,
    full_name: {type: String?, generated: true}
  )
end
```
